{"conditionalSetIds":[],"forecasterId":"demographics-ai-2036:run1:growth:outside","informationSetArtifactId":"sha256:e13d625915038154bebe3e18f64643c104666282459e574d126a08e423deaa51","modelForecastIds":[],"modelUse":"none","prediction":{"kind":"quantiles","quantiles":[{"probability":0.1,"value":0.7},{"probability":0.5,"value":1.9},{"probability":0.9,"value":3}]},"questionHash":"sha256:e1b53adbdbe1bb1ccef339035b6376b49668b9341050c684f480d5b347e316f6","questionId":"demographics-ai-2036-cq3","questionVersion":"1.0.0","rationale":"Mandated reference class: 5-year windows of OECD+China PPP-weighted real growth since 1960. My reconstruction of the block (OECD-38 + China, PPP-weighted): 1960s ~5.0%, 1970s ~4.0%, 1980s ~4.0%, 1990s ~3.6%, 2000s ~3.2%, 2010-2019 ~3.2%, 2015-2019 ~3.4%, 2020-2024 ~2.6%. Mapped to the CQ6 bands, the unconditional distribution is brutal for my own answer: roughly 85-90% of all rolling 5-year windows since 1965 landed >=2.6% (band A territory), ~12-15% landed <2.0% (the 1979-83, 2008-12 and 2020-24 clusters), and essentially NONE landed below 1.5%. Pure outside view with no adjustment would give P50 ~3.0%. I depart from that by ~110bp, and the entire departure is arithmetic rather than mechanism, which is what my lens permits. Two pieces. (1) PPP weights. The block is ~64-65% of world PPP GDP; within it China is ~30% and OECD-38 ~70% today, drifting to roughly China 32% / OECD 68% by 2032-2036. China's contribution to the block's growth was 0.30*10.0 = 3.0 pp in the 2000s at a smaller weight; at a 32% weight and a 3.0% growth rate it contributes 0.96 pp. That single reweighting-plus-deceleration arithmetic removes ~1.5-2.0 pp from the block relative to every pre-2015 window in the reference class, and it is why the unconditional base rate is non-stationary here. The locked prior's -2.7 pp/yr China aging drag times a ~0.32 PPP weight is ~-0.86 pp on the block, the largest single term in the answer. (2) OECD arithmetic. The locked prior gives OECD baseline real GDP per capita at 0.6%/yr for 2036 (down from 1.0%) absent productivity offsets; OECD population growth is ~0.2-0.25%/yr and falling. Mechanical OECD GDP growth = ~0.85%/yr. I mark that UP to 1.2% because the historical base rate of a 5-year OECD window with zero productivity offset is low -- the lowest non-crisis OECD 5-year averages are ~1.3% -- and because 2A itself books a +0.4 pp/yr healthy-aging offset. Build-up: 0.68*1.2 + 0.32*3.0 = 0.82 + 0.96 = 1.78%. China's 3.0% is itself a reference-class number, not a forecast: post-catch-up decelerations at China's income level ran Japan 1991-1996 ~1.4%, Korea 2012-2019 ~2.9%, Taiwan 2000-2010 ~4.0%. Spread. OECD sigma ~0.66 (P10/P90 0.4/2.1), China sigma ~1.41 (P10/P90 1.0/4.6), weighted contributions 0.45 and 0.45, rho ~0.5 -> block sigma = sqrt(0.2025+0.2025+0.2025) = 0.78. Symmetric P10/P90 would be 0.81/2.81. I skew the distribution: the downside is fattened because the locked AI-Q4 marginal (66% impairment/revenue shortfall in 2027-2032) means a capex-bust recession is more likely than not somewhere in the run-up, and a 5-year window that contains the digestion phase prints 60-100bp below trend; the upside is fattened slightly less, by the healthy-aging offset and the possibility that the OECD 0.6% baseline is simply too pessimistic versus its own history. Final: P10 0.7 / P50 1.9 / P90 3.0. I flag explicitly that P10 = 0.7% has NO precedent in the reference class -- no 5-year window of this block since 1960 has printed below ~1.3% -- and I am assigning it 10% purely on the China-weight arithmetic.","referenceClassIds":[],"schemaVersion":"forecast-workbench.forecast/v1","sealedAt":"2026-11-04T17:31:54.058Z","seriesId":"published-panel","strongestContraryCase":"Not elicited per question. Panelist falsification signposts: {\"signpost\":\"OECD area harmonised unemployment rate (FRED OECDLRHUTTTTSTSAQ / OECD MEI) printing a 12-month average at or below 4.6% with no OECD-area recession dated in the interval -- i.e. the demographic labour-scarcity floor arriving faster than the cyclical reference class allows.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2029\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"CQ1 conjunct A moves from 45% toward 65-70%, lifting CQ1 from 16 to roughly 24-26. It also raises CQ2 conjunct A (wage-driven services inflation) by ~5 points and shifts CQ6 mass from D toward B and C by ~4 points combined. It does NOT move CQ3, since tighter labour with a shrinking headcount is the same output.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"A full 5-year realised print of PPP-weighted (OECD-38 + China) real GDP growth for 2026-2030 at or above 2.6%/yr on IMF WEO constant-PPP data, with China's own 2028-2030 average at or above 4.0%/yr.\",\"by_when\":\"Apr 30, 2032 (IMF WEO publishing the 2031 actual)\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"This is the cleanest falsifier of my central claim, because it would show the China-weight-times-deceleration arithmetic I leaned on by ~110bp is not binding. CQ3 P50 moves from 1.9% to ~2.5% and the whole distribution shifts up ~60bp; CQ6-A moves from 19 to ~34, B stays ~47, C and D fall to ~11 and ~8. My entire departure from the 85-90% historical base rate for band A would be invalidated.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"The G7 GDP-weighted 10y sovereign real yield (US DFII10 plus euro-area, UK, Japan and Canada 10y nominal less 10y inflation swap) averaging below 1.0% over any rolling 24 months, with US net interest outlays still at or above 3% of GDP in the same period.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2031\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Falsifies my persistence-plus-supply-tilt anchor: it would show the debt arithmetic does not transmit to real yields (convenience yield intact, or a renewed central-bank duration bid). CQ4 P50 moves from 2.1% to ~1.3% and P90 from 3.4% to ~2.6%; CQ5 conjunct A (MOVE >=105) falls from 45% to ~32%, dragging CQ5 from 33 to ~24; CQ6-D rises ~5 points at C's expense. Asset matrix: G7 long nominal bonds in regime C would need marking up and gold marking down.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"An MSCI World peak-to-trough decline of 25% or more during which G7 GDP-weighted 10y nominal yields fall by 100bp or more -- i.e. the flight-to-quality bid working cleanly through a large drawdown in a post-2026 high-debt, high-real-rate setting.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2031\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Consumes the most likely qualifying episode of the window with a NO. CQ5 conjunct B falls from 48% to roughly 28% (only a later, second drawdown could qualify), taking CQ5 from 33 to ~20. It also raises the equity-bond diversification value underpinning my regime-C/regime-D bond spread, narrowing the C-vs-D gap for G7 long nominals from 10 pp to ~6 pp.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"OECD or Eurostat structural business/earnings statistics showing cumulative real median hourly earnings growth in ISIC F+Q exceeding ISIC J+K+M by 5.0 pp or more over 2025-2031 across a majority of the OECD-38 by GDP weight (i.e. half the required 11-year differential delivered in six years).\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2032 (on data through 2031)\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"CQ1 conjunct B moves from 22% to ~55%, taking CQ1 from 16 to roughly 30-33. It also raises CQ6-B at A's and D's expense (the barbell's defining wage signature) by ~5 points, and raises the locked AI-Q5 marginal's implied value further above 38.2%. Note this signpost's absence by 2032 would NOT confirm my view, since the differential could be back-loaded.\"}","updateBasis":{"kind":"launch","note":"run1 wave 1 outside seat, effort 'growth'."}}